the first Mirror's Edge was a painfully  flawed game but during those fleeting  moments where everything works as  intended there was nothing quite likely  few games can match it for evoking a  sense of blissful freedom it was almost  as though you could feel the wind sail  through you as you dashed and bolted and  clambered across its rooftops an air  vent water pipes it's these memories  that make me so encouraged about the  game you're looking at now Mirror's Edge  catalyst VA's long-awaited reboot of  this distinct first person parkour  adventure because here that zen-like  feeling of empowerment and momentum that  sense that you are the master of your  elements is very much still alive but  all the floors the came of the first  game they appear to be gone  chief among the emissions is the  firearms halfway through the first  message the game just sort of gave up  being what it wanted to be and hand you  a lame automatic pistol however at no  point in catalyst we are told will you  enact your second Amendment right  overcoming foes is achieved with melee  attacks driven by momentum the faster  and further you run the harder you hit  mixing melee attacks with jumps and  dashes and slides the order of the day  but you have to keep moving if you find  yourself ruling of someone from a static  standing position the fight will likely  be awkward graceless and deliberately  tedious  murs edge catus demands you move as fast  as possible in fact if you build up  enough speed of momentum eventually your  enter state where you automatically  dodge bullets the on-screen hints of  where to go next meanwhile come via  dashes of red painless speed off into  the distance it's almost as if the game  is telling you to keep half of its  directions  catalyst has also adopted an open world  mission structure much like in Grand  Theft Auto games you encounter people  across the city of glass to find out  what missions they have for you some are  main characters who present key  objectives that drive the narrative  forwards other our random people  scattered across the rooftops you know  just hanging out who offer various side  quests for gamer hinges so much on  exploration it's a no-brainer that dice  opened up the world the main mission  stick to the basics one moment you'll be  breaking into a building and hacking  into a computer terminal the next you'll  be fleeing from armed helicopters in  pursuit the side quests tend to ask you  to deliver a package or reach a certain  point in the map within a certain  timeframe the tight time constraints  place on you demand flawless freerunning  and make such challenges exhilarating on  top of this is a new feature that allows  you to create your own time trials for  other players to complete you place  markers and checkpoints across the city  and upload that route online on paper it  seems like a bullet point edition a way  for dice to shoehorn in an online  feature in practice it's utterly  absorbing stripping the game down to the  fundamental joy of fighting against the  clock by running as efficiently and  creatively as possible I suppose that  shouldn't be a surprise free running  after all has always been the  fundamental joy of this series for every  annoyance that catalyst appears to have  removed it's so encouraging to see that  dice completely understands what fans  love about the original Mirror's Edge  catalyst is due to ship on May 24 from  ps4 xbox one and pc you can head to game  sort right now for an in-depth preview  with a full breakdown of the games  you
 

